A53 — the vblank grid is the panel's latch grid

gamescope's vblank timer steps on the nested refresh and re-anchors on every
page-flip timestamp. Under gamenative-drm both were fictions: the app passed
-r 1200, and the daemon stamped each flip on its own 1200 Hz timeline, whose
phase is set by the first flip and has nothing to do with SurfaceFlinger's
latch. The compositor woke ~1200-1500 times a second, and the frame limiter
(A52) released frames on ticks that sat at an arbitrary, drifting phase
against the panel's 8.3 ms latches.

With -r at the panel rate, this asks gamenative-drm for page-flip stamps on
SurfaceFlinger's latch grid (client cap GNDRM_CLIENT_CAP_LATCH_GRID,
0x474E4C47 "GNLG"): each flip is stamped with the latch it lands on minus the
daemon's publish lead. MarkVBlank already re-anchors the timer on that stamp,
so the timer now wakes once per panel latch, a fixed lead before it, and the
limiter's releases, the frame callbacks and the paints all sit at a fixed
phase against the latch. Nothing else in the timer changes: it was already an
absolute CLOCK_MONOTONIC timerfd.

- An older daemon refuses the cap (EINVAL): one log line, the stamps stay on
  its own timeline, and the timer still runs at the panel rate (no phase
  lock).
- A session launched on the legacy fine grid (-r above 500) does not ask for
  the cap and ignores gn_panel_refresh, so an old app with a new daemon keeps
  today's behaviour exactly.
- The panel's rate can change while the session runs (120 <-> 60). The daemon
  posts it as the ConVar gn_panel_refresh <Hz> over gamescope_private; the
  steamcompmgr thread moves the nested refresh to it at the top of its loop,
  re-anchors the limiter and re-arms the timer on the new grid.
- The limited refresh cycle handed to clients (presentation feedback) was a
  whole number of vblanks, refresh / target, which truncates on a coarse grid
  (40 fps on 60 Hz read as one vblank, 16.7 ms instead of 25). The limiter
  releases on a time grid since A52, so the cycle is now the target's period.
- A gamescope_private command repaints once (force_repaint): scaling_filter,
  scaling_sharpness and debug_force_repaint change the next frame with no
  commit behind them.

Log lines (logcat, System.out "[gamescope]"):
  drm: vblank: daemon latch-grid stamps on (GNLG accepted, grid N mHz)
  drm: vblank: daemon latch-grid stamps off (GNLG rejected: ...)
  drm: vblank: daemon latch-grid stamps off (legacy -r grid N mHz)
  vblank: gn_panel_refresh applied: vblank grid N mHz (was M mHz)

diff --git a/src/Backends/DRMBackend.cpp b/src/Backends/DRMBackend.cpp
index de5960f..a9e0542 100644
--- a/src/Backends/DRMBackend.cpp
+++ b/src/Backends/DRMBackend.cpp
@@ -1602,6 +1602,31 @@ bool init_drm(struct drm_t *drm, int width, int height, int refresh)
 		return false;
 	}
 
+	// gamenative-drm: ask for page-flip timestamps on SurfaceFlinger's latch grid
+	// (the latch a flip lands on, minus the daemon's publish lead) instead of the
+	// daemon's own fixed-rate timeline. MarkVBlank anchors the vblank timer on those
+	// stamps, so with -r at the panel rate the compositor wakes once per latch, a fixed
+	// lead before it. A session on the legacy fine grid (-r far above any panel) keeps
+	// the daemon's timeline it was built around. An older daemon refuses the cap
+	// (EINVAL) and stamps as before.
+	if ( BUsesGameNativeDrm() )
+	{
+		static constexpr uint64_t GNDRM_CLIENT_CAP_LATCH_GRID = 0x474E4C47ull; // "GNLG"
+		static constexpr int k_nMaxPanelRefreshmHz = 500'000;
+		if ( refresh > k_nMaxPanelRefreshmHz )
+		{
+			drm_log.infof( "vblank: daemon latch-grid stamps off (legacy -r grid %d mHz)", refresh );
+		}
+		else if ( drmSetClientCap( drm->fd, GNDRM_CLIENT_CAP_LATCH_GRID, 1 ) == 0 )
+		{
+			drm_log.infof( "vblank: daemon latch-grid stamps on (GNLG accepted, grid %d mHz)", refresh );
+		}
+		else
+		{
+			drm_log.infof( "vblank: daemon latch-grid stamps off (GNLG rejected: %s)", strerror( errno ) );
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (drmGetCap(drm->fd, DRM_CAP_CURSOR_WIDTH, &drm->cursor_width) != 0) {
 		drm->cursor_width = 64;
 	}
diff --git a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
index d6da2ad..1ef5d95 100644
--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -1279,6 +1279,32 @@ static void steamcompmgr_update_fps_limit_release( uint64_t ulTargetVBlank )
 	if ( g_ulFPSLimitNextRelease <= ulTargetVBlank + ulVBlank / 2 )
 		g_ulFPSLimitNextRelease = ulTargetVBlank + ulPeriod;
 }
+
+// The panel's refresh while the session runs. gamenative-drm posts it through
+// gamescope_private when the panel changes rate (120 <-> 60); the launch value is -r.
+// The session's vblank grid is the nested refresh: with the daemon's latch-grid flip
+// stamps the timer then wakes once per panel latch, so the frame limiter releases on
+// real latches. A session launched on the legacy fine grid (-r far above any panel)
+// keeps that grid: the daemon stamps it on its own timeline, and moving it to a panel
+// rate with no phase lock would change pacing for that build pair.
+static constexpr int k_nMaxPanelRefreshHz = 500;
+
+static gamescope::ConVar<int> cv_gn_panel_refresh( "gn_panel_refresh", 0, "GameNative: the panel's refresh in Hz; moves the vblank grid (nested refresh) to it.",
+[]( gamescope::ConVar<int> &cv )
+{
+	if ( cv <= 0 || cv > k_nMaxPanelRefreshHz )
+	{
+		xwm_log.infof( "gn_panel_refresh %d ignored: out of range", cv.Get() );
+		return;
+	}
+	if ( g_nNestedRefresh > gamescope::ConvertHztomHz( k_nMaxPanelRefreshHz ) )
+	{
+		xwm_log.infof( "gn_panel_refresh %d ignored: session runs the legacy %d mHz grid", cv.Get(), g_nNestedRefresh );
+		return;
+	}
+	GetVBlankTimer().RequestRefreshmHz( gamescope::ConvertHztomHz( cv.Get() ) );
+	nudge_steamcompmgr();
+});
 static uint64_t g_uDynamicRefreshEqualityTime = 0;
 static int g_nDynamicRefreshRate[gamescope::GAMESCOPE_SCREEN_TYPE_COUNT] = { 0, 0 };
